diagnosis_text:
A thorough multi-phase investigation of the simple_bgp topology found no fault. Phase 1 confirmed that pc1 (195.11.14.2) can reach pc2 (200.1.1.2) with 0% packet loss. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) revealed no ACL rules, no addressing anomalies, no missing IPs or default routes, and no duplicate MAC addresses on any device. Phase B confirmed that both FRR routers are healthy — watchfrr, zebra, and bgpd are all active; the eBGP session between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) is Established with exactly one connection established and zero dropped; no traffic-control shaping (tc_snapshot all fq_codel at defaults) is present. Direct inspection of the router1:eth0 ↔ router2:eth0 inter-company link showed zero errors, zero drops, and zero carrier errors on both ends. The carrier_changes counter reads 2 on every interface across both routers, which matches the normal Kathara startup baseline (one veth DOWN→UP transition at container creation). Phase D (pressure_sweep) found no stress tools, flap scripts, or CPU/socket anomalies on any device. No BGP NOTIFICATION messages have been exchanged, and dhcp_link_history found no suspicious link-recovery events. Journalctl returned no BGP-, link-, or flap-related log entries. In summary, there is no mechanism — no flap process, no elevated carrier counter, no BGP session drop history, no tc fault — that could produce the reported intermittent drops. The user's symptom is unconfirmed by any live or historical evidence. Root cause: there is no injected fault; the network is operating normally and the reported intermittent behaviour is not reproducible in the current lab state.
